INT. DRIED-OUT PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

Dome overhead. Cracked seats. Projector dust under a single work light.

DETECTIVE IMOGEN SALAZAR, 40s, crouches by a chalk outline. Her partner, DET. REYNALD KOWALSKI, 50s, eats sunflower seeds. CURATOR ASTRID LUNDGREN, 60s, stands arms folded. SECURITY GUARD BASHIR HALLOWAY, 30s, lingers by the door. A voice crackles on Imogen's radio.

IMOGEN
Nobody touched the projector?

ASTRID
Nobody has touched it in years.

REYNALD
Then who turned it on?

BASHIR
Wasn't me. I was at the front desk.

ASTRID
He was at the desk. I saw him.

REYNALD
You saw him at the desk at ten. Victim died at ten-thirty.

BASHIR
I was at the desk until eleven.

IMOGEN
Quiet, all of you.

The room goes still. Imogen presses the radio.

CORONER (V.O.)
(over radio)
Imogen, the stars on his jacket. They're real stars. Pins. Constellation of Orion.

IMOGEN
Orion?

ASTRID
Orion is the show we never finished.

The projector hums to life. Above them, the dome blooms with stars. Orion rises. Bashir takes a step back.

REYNALD
Who's in the booth?

ASTRID
No one. It's automatic.

BASHIR
It's not automatic. Not anymore.

IMOGEN
Bashir. How would you know that?

Bashir does not answer. The stars drift. Reynald draws his weapon. Astrid draws a breath.

ASTRID
It was supposed to be a tribute.

IMOGEN
To who?

ASTRID
To the man in the outline. Yours truly built that show. I built it for him.

Reynald lowers the gun a few inches, then raises it again, pointing at Astrid.

REYNALD
Ma'am, step away from the console.
